From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <jschwender@posteo.net>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 14:05:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: make the disk the primary HTTP cache

Add `DiskStore`, a file-per-entry backend behind `CacheStore`, and turn it on by
default wherever the embedder gives Servo a cache directory. Cached bodies then
live only on disk, the OS page cache is the memory tier, and the net process
holds nothing but an index.

Layout, following Chromium's Simple Cache: a version stamp whose mismatch
discards the directory, an index written through a temporary file and a rename,
and one file per variant under `entries/`. There is no fsync anywhere -- neither
Chromium nor Firefox does one, and `fdatasync` costs 8 ms on eMMC.

The entry format puts the metadata *after* the body:

    magic u64 | format u32 | flags u32
    body, exactly as received
    meta, postcard(EntryMeta)
    meta_len u32 | meta_crc u32 | body_crc u32 | body_len u64 | eof magic u64

That is the one deliberate departure from a header-first layout: freshening an
entry after a 304 replaces metadata whose length changes, and with the metadata
at the end that is a truncate and an append instead of rewriting the body. A
file without a valid trailer was never committed and is ignored, so a crash
mid-write costs nothing. The body checksum is verified only on a read that runs
to EOF, as in Chromium; a ranged read skips it.

The index holds `{key_hash, slot, size, last_used}` and nothing else, so the RAM
per entry stays at Chromium's scale; headers and policy are read from the entry
file on lookup, one tail read that the page cache serves after the first time.
It is a derived artifact throughout: a bad checksum, a missing file or a
directory that changed underneath the cache all repair by scanning, which is
what makes the store survive OpenHarmony emptying `cacheDir` at any instant. An
entry whose body does not read back is unlinked rather than left in place, so a
file the page cache happens to have lost cannot keep failing the same URL.

All blocking I/O goes to one dedicated thread per store, QoS-boosted onto the
larger cores. The unit of hand-off is a batch of up to 256 KiB rather than a
64 KiB chunk, because the measured cross-thread hand-off is 28 to 45 us on the
DAYU200 and the PLR -- several times the 64 KiB read it would dispatch -- so an
entry under 256 KiB, which is most of the web, costs exactly one hand-off.
Reads keep one batch in flight while the previous one is decoded. `tokio::fs`
was rejected for costing a hand-off per operation, inline I/O on a tokio worker
for stalling it on a 375 us cold read, and mmap because a fault over a 64 KiB
span costs 28 to 36 percent of a read that also copies the data.

Sizing follows the plan: 100 MiB on OpenHarmony and Android, Chromium's
free-space ladder elsewhere (`statvfs`, no new dependency), both overridable
through `network_http_disk_cache_size`. Entries are capped at an eighth of the
budget and at 20 000 files, since inodes are metered per uid on OpenHarmony.
`Opts::http_cache_dir` carries the location; servoshell defaults it to
`<cacheDir>/servo/http-cache` on Android and OpenHarmony and to
`dirs::cache_dir()/servo/http-cache` elsewhere. `--temporary-storage` and
private browsing stay on the memory store, so WPT runs are unaffected.

`about:memory` gains `http-cache/index` for the in-process index and a non-heap
`http-cache/disk` for the bytes on disk.

Persisting the index is part of being disk-primary, because on a phone the
process is almost never shut down cleanly -- it is frozen and then killed -- so
an index that is only written on a timer is rarely written at all, and every
cold start pays a directory scan (74 ms at 20 000 entries on the PLR). Measured
on device: after `aa force-stop`, `shutdown()` never runs. Chromium solves this
the same way and this follows it: `SimpleIndex` uses
`kWriteToDiskDelayMSecs = 20000` in the foreground and
`kWriteToDiskOnBackgroundDelayMSecs = 100` in the background, and
`OnApplicationStateChange()` -- registered through `ApplicationStatusListener` --
writes the index immediately on `APPLICATION_STATE_HAS_STOPPED_ACTIVITIES`.

  * The index flush is a real debounce: 20 s after the *last* change, not "at
    most every 20 s while active", matching Chromium's `PostponeWritingToDisk`.
    A generation counter means a burst of commits costs one index write, and an
    application that goes quiet still persists.

  * `NetworkManager::notify_application_backgrounded()` is a new embedder API
    that flushes immediately, wired on OpenHarmony from
    `EntryAbility.onBackground()` through a new `onApplicationBackground` napi
    export. It is deliberately a general embedder API rather than an
    OpenHarmony detail: Android's `onTrimMemory` / `ApplicationStatusListener`
    is the same signal, and an embedded webview can forward its host's
    equivalent.

    The call *waits* for the flush, bounded by `BACKGROUND_FLUSH_TIMEOUT`
    (500 ms), rather than enqueuing it: the application can be frozen once the
    callback returns, so firing and forgetting would race the freeze. The bound
    exists only so a busy Servo thread cannot stall the ability's main thread;
    hitting it is logged and means something else is wrong, since the work is a
    few kilobytes with no `fsync`.

    No background permission is needed, and deliberately so.
    `ohos.permission.KEEP_BACKGROUND_RUNNING` covers *continuous* tasks, which
    this is not. The other option is a `backgroundTaskManager` transient task
    via `requestSuspendDelay`, which needs no permission and may be requested
    during `onBackground` -- its documented use case is exactly "saving state on
    app exit". It is still the wrong tool: transient tasks are quota'd (at most
    3 per time segment and 10 minutes a day) and sized for work measured in
    seconds, so spending one on a sub-millisecond index write would burn a
    budget a real background job may need. If this API ever grows a heavier
    user -- a cache trim driven by a memory-pressure signal, say -- a transient
    task becomes the right wrapper, and that is when to add it.

  * Index staleness is decided by a dirty flag in the version stamp, which is
    Firefox's approach ("set as soon as the index is read and cleared only when
    the shutdown journal has been written successfully"). A directory mtime
    cannot tell an unclean exit from a clean one. The mtime check stays
    *alongside* the flag, because the flag only covers our own exit while the
    mtime catches the system emptying the cache directory while Servo is not
    running -- both have to hold before the index is trusted.

`index::load()` also gained `http-cache: index loaded from disk` /
`index rebuilt by scanning` markers. The two are indistinguishable from
behaviour alone -- a rebuild finds the same entries, so the cache answers either
way -- so without them nothing can assert that a flush actually saved a scan.

mmap for the index was considered and rejected: it would survive SIGKILL, but it
offers no atomicity, so a kill mid-update leaves a torn record, which is harder
to detect than the current single CRC over the whole payload and would need a
fixed-slot format plus per-record validation. The lifecycle hook covers the case
that actually happens for a fraction of the risk.

Validation: (1) `./mach test-unit -p servo-net` -- 437 tests, all passing.
`tests/cache_store.rs` runs one suite against both stores through a macro
(commit/read, abort, multi-batch bodies, variants, ranges, `update_meta`,
byte-budget eviction, remove/clear, concurrent writers, descriptors), so a
behaviour only one backend gets right fails there. Three tests pin the index
semantics: an unclean exit still finds its entries, a flush makes the index
reusable, and entries removed while Servo was closed are *not* served from an
otherwise-clean index.
(2) The disk-specific half of that file is the hostile suite the design asks
for: entries survive a restart, a corrupt index is rebuilt from the files, a
temporary file left by a killed writer is cleaned up, an entry file removed
behind the store's back reads as a miss, the whole directory can be deleted
while the store runs and it recreates it, a foreign version stamp discards the
directory, a flipped body byte surfaces as an error rather than silent
corruption, a forged hash collision does not serve the wrong URL, and an
outsized entry is declined. A corrupt body is also checked to be *dropped*, not
just reported, and no credential from the request reaches the entry file.
(3) On PLR-AL00, release, `--flavor=harmonyos`: after loading a page no
`index-data` exists at 12 s (the debounce has not fired); pressing Home produces
it within 2 s; the stamp's clean byte reads 1; no "background timeout" warning
appears in hilog, so the flush finished well inside the bound; and the next
start serves the whole page with zero server requests, from the trusted index
rather than a scan. With a page that keeps fetching, the clean byte correctly
reverts to 0 and the next start rescans -- the safe direction. Process PSS over
three alternating pairs: 234.9 -> 181.8 MB, -53.0 MB (-22.6%), all of it
anonymous.
(4) `./mach check`, `./mach check --ohos` warning-free; `./mach test-tidy` clean.
(5) After a rebase, re-check `Opts` and the servoshell argument plumbing; re-run
the hostile suite, which is the only thing pinning the "no store error ever
fails a fetch" invariant; and re-check that `EntryAbility.onBackground()` still
calls in and still *waits*. That last one is a one-line call site a conflict can
silently drop, and making it fire-and-forget again restores the race -- nothing
in the Rust build notices either.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implementation and testing)
